{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of MVP S3 at head bfca55ea6f77970ae94862d375170e0f0c8eeea1, tree 0f5ab2e4, for request 7951d998 and review request 0cc80f1d. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for one defect in the definition.\n\nThe head adds two commits to main 5068dab4. I read the implementation note first, then the definition, the pin and reference scripts, both tests, the generated reference and the evidence. I found the defect by reading the definition against `packages/derive/src/outcomes.ts`. I didn't reproduce it at runtime.\n\nEverything the request's witnesses name holds. The one gap is what happens to an approval request after the text moves on.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. An open approval request can be stranded forever, and stranded requests fill the type.\n   - `withdraw-approval-request` guards on `{ state: [\"asked\"], of: \"also.ask\" }` and on the request's `bodyAt` equalling the ask's current `bodyAt`. `approve` carries the same two guards. So once the ask is revised, or decided, or withdrawn, nothing can close an open request made on the earlier text. The scenario's own `agentRequest` and `oldPersonRequest` stay open to the end.\n   - `approval-request` has `max: 64`, and `outcomes.ts:210` says \"`max` bounds the live items of a type\", refused as `type-full`. An open request is live. So stranded requests count for good, and the 65th live request in that issue is refused, for every question in the issue, with no act that can free the room.\n   - Scenario: an asker requests a review, revises the text while the reviewer is away, and repeats. Revising during a review is ordinary use. A member holding `issue.comment` can also do it on purpose, because a request needs only that grant, and `request-exists` is per body and requested member.\n   - The same requests also stay \"open requests to me\" for their reviewers, who can't act on them. S4's `needs_you` is filed to read exactly that state.\n   - The decision guards are right to ignore them. The defect is only that nothing can close them.\n   - Fix: let the requester withdraw a request bound to its own `bodyAt` whatever has happened to the ask, by removing the ask-state guard and the ask-current equality from `withdraw-approval-request`. Keep `state: open`, the requester signer and the equality with the request's own `bodyAt`, which keeps the act bound to its text as the request asks. Add assertions that a stale request and a request on a decided ask can be withdrawn, and that a stranger can't. The repin needs a new full gate.\n   - If the planner prefers another shape, such as a `revise-ask` that closes current requests, the question goes to the planner.\n\n## Against the request\n\n1. Existing forms and preserved pins. Resolved.\n   - The successor is a cloned declared definition with its own canonical file, digest and export. `definitions.test` and the new whole-definition test show that historical bytes and unrelated rows are unchanged. No kernel, Worker, contract or Page path changes. `pin.mjs --questions` writes only the successor.\n   - It sits at the bounds' edge: 14 of 14 item types is enforced, by a case that lowers the bound by one and fails.\n2. Binding. Resolved apart from item 1.\n   - A request binds the ask, its body fact and the requested member. `approve` needs the requested signer, an open request, and the request and the ask both on the supplied body. The asker can't approve, and withdrawal supplies no verdict.\n   - `decide` needs the current body, no current open request, no current `request-changes` and at least one current `approve`. Older verdicts stay readable and count only for their own body.\n   - `release-block` compares the decision fact's scope and `on` with the blocked ask, so another ask's genuine decision is refused `not-this-ask`.\n3. The Native journey. Resolved.\n   - It runs actual Membership8, Rules and Directory, with empty caller grants. It enrolls people by real invitation proofs and a true controlled agent, and it reads back kinds, roles, controllers and key bindings.\n   - All six witnesses assert their refusal and a successful counterpart, with the head unchanged around each refusal and detached text read back.\n   - The role boundary is what `set-actions` configures. Only person role lists gain `issue.approve` and `issue.decide`. The note says plainly that this doesn't protect against deliberately granting a person role to an agent.\n4. Controls. Resolved. The two controls omit the current-body and open-request guards, repin only the successor and fail by the intended assertions, with exact restoration. Neither one covers the withdraw guards.\n\n## The gates\n\nThe gate at dc27a154, tree 672c4596, passes all five stages. Its receipt exits 0 with complete capture and unchanged source. Plain passes 3,146 with one skip and Native 860 with two skips. From dc27a154 to the head, only the note and the manifest change. Item 1 changes the pinned definition, so a new full gate is due at the next head.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 5068dab4 gives the head tree 0f5ab2e4.\n- The note has SHA-256 74338b33 and the manifest 26d82eca, as the request says. The eleven artifacts name the eleven changed paths.\n- I ran `issue-questions.test.ts` and the native `issue-questions.scope.test.ts` once each: 1 of 1 and 1 of 1. The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- The evidence verifies. All 145 cited files match, the directory has 237 files and 77,322,503 bytes, its `SHA256SUMS` has digest 48fc9f76 and reruns clean, and no file is unlisted or writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken. check-notes: 0 warnings. check-commit-messages: 2 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `approval` has `max: 256`, and its only state `submitted` is not final, so verdicts in one issue stop at 256 for good. This fails closed, and it's large for one issue. It may be worth a note.\n2. `withdraw-ask` leaves requests open, and it needs no change beyond item 1. A block on a withdrawn ask has no release through `decide`. That matches the old lane's behaviour, but the block's owner needs another way out.\n3. `docs/lanes-reference.md` shows the grants belong on person roles, but nothing checks it at activation. S3 stays people only by configuration, as the request says.\n\n## Architecture\n\nAn opt-in successor under existing forms leaves older rooms alone, and the kernel and Workers stay untouched. That is the right shape for this slice. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe authority rules hold: only a requested signer with the grant can approve, the asker can't, and a decision needs current text. Item 1 lets any holder of `issue.comment` exhaust a type for good, which denies approval requests to everyone in the issue. Changes requested, item 1.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe definition adds five acts over one current-text rule. Item 1's fix removes two guards. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: changes requested, item 1.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: conditions 1 and 3 to 7 hold. Condition 2 holds apart from item 1.\n\nA head that makes this change can come back for a review of its diff from this head. No deployment is due from this source: no Worker serves the definition.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:69b5000341408b832ee64b1bbad00a2cfdd9fbfb","binding":"assigned","head":"bfca55ea6f77970ae94862d375170e0f0c8eeea1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a5c4075740c1d21f334020bf1b3f8ea785f7ae68\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2800149a8a445ab650cc1c7b8f18820ec603a7a0\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a5c4075740c1d21f334020bf1b3f8ea785f7ae68","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}